# Git Rebase and Merge

## Git Rebase

Git rebase is used to move or replay the commits of one branch onto another branch.

It rewrites the commit history by placing the feature branch commits on top of the latest commits of the target branch.

## Important Point

Rebase creates a linear project history.

It is mainly used to avoid unnecessary merge commits and keep the commit history clean.

---

# Git Merge --no-ff

Normally, when a fast-forward merge is possible, Git simply moves the branch pointer forward.

Using:

git merge --no-ff branch-name

forces Git to create a merge commit even when a fast-forward merge is possible.

## Why use --no-ff?

A merge commit preserves the history of the feature branch.

It helps us understand:

- When a feature branch was merged.
- Which commits belonged to that feature.
- The complete branch history.

---

# Checking Commit History

## To check commits of a particular branch

git log --oneline branch-name

## To check the commit history as a graph

git log --oneline --graph

This shows the branch structure and merge history.

---

# Rebase Concept

Suppose we have:

main:

A --- B

feature branch:

A --- B --- C --- D

After rebasing, the feature branch commits are replayed on top of the latest commits of the target branch.

The commit history becomes more linear.

Rebase is useful for keeping the project history clean.

---

# Important Notes

- Git rebase rewrites commit history.
- Rebase creates a linear history.
- Git merge combines branches.
- git merge --no-ff creates a merge commit.
- --no-ff is useful for preserving feature branch history.
- git log --oneline branch-name shows commits of a branch.
- git log --oneline --graph shows the commit history as a graph.
